WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
Why counting runs out of road.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:04.056
The bigram model sees one character.

3
00:00:04.336 --> 00:00:16.155
To see more, you could count longer contexts: a table indexed by the previous three characters has 27 cubed, or 19,683, rows of 27 entries each.

4
00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:26.504
Most of those contexts never occur in 32,033 names, so most rows would be empty or nearly so, and the estimates in the rest would be noisy.

5
00:00:26.784 --> 00:00:31.285
Every extra character of context multiplies the table by 27.

6
00:00:31.565 --> 00:00:42.019
This is the curse of dimensionality for discrete models, and it’s the problem Bengio and colleagues set out to fix in “A Neural Probabilistic Language Model” (2003).

7
00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:58.192
Their idea, which the lecture walks through at 01:48, is to map each token to a learned vector in a low-dimensional space, an embedding, and to predict the next token with a neural network that reads the concatenated embeddings of the context.

8
00:00:58.472 --> 00:01:06.771
Similar tokens can end up with similar vectors, so evidence about one context transfers to contexts the model has never seen.

9
00:01:07.051 --> 00:01:12.064
The paper did this for words; the lecture does it for characters, with a context of three.

10
00:01:12.344 --> 00:01:20.728
This is the conceptual ancestor of every embedding you met in Studio 2, and of the token embedding table at the bottom of GPT.
